Here is the feedback we got for generated code for data_preprocess.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Fix feature universe construction. Your temporary write of gene_index.json causes determine_feature_universe to treat all genes as “prior genes,” so you never reduce to a focused feature set. Instead:
  - Do not write any temporary artifacts to PRIOR_OUTPUT_DIR.
  - Build feature_genes = HVGs ∪ curated-set genes (rows with ≥1 membership in geneset_membership.npz aligned to current var_names) ∪ marker genes (marker_sets.json), but only if those prior files actually exist; otherwise ignore that source. Subset before reconciling/saving priors. This will cut noise features and typically improves ARI.

- Canonical gene symbol harmonization. Uppercasing + keep-first drops signal and harms overlap with priors:
  - Map dataset genes to HGNC canonical symbols (e.g., using meta_info.csv/synonyms), then collapse duplicates by summing counts across duplicate symbols (sparse-safe aggregation) rather than dropping them. This boosts prior overlap and HVG fidelity, improving cluster quality.

- Implement planned low-value gene handling. Beyond MT- removal:
  - Drop rRNA and obvious pseudogenes only if flagged by meta_info; otherwise retain but downweight.
  - Exclude housekeeping genes (RPL*, RPS*, MALAT1) from HVGs explicitly (set var['highly_variable']=False) while keeping them in the model with low loss weight. This reduces nuisance variance dominating the encoder and tends to raise ARI.

- Strengthen prior curation during reconciliation (after aligning to the current gene list):
  - Filter sets to size in [10, 300] and require retained_fraction ≥ 0.5 (compute from original vs retained members).
  - Deduplicate near-identical sets using Jaccard > 0.8, preferring MSigDB > GO > NeST.
  - Recompute TF-IDF and the gene graph from the pruned membership. This reduces collinearity and yields crisper clusters.

- Recompute normalization after subsetting. After selecting feature_genes, recompute layers['log1p_cpm'] using subset library sizes and save per-cell size_factor in obs. Aligning encoder input scaling with the decoder’s size factors stabilizes training and can improve ARI.

- Make stratified splits robust. Before calling train_test_split with stratify, verify each stratum has enough samples for val/test; if not, merge rare strata or fall back to random splits with the same seed. Preventing invalid stratification avoids brittle validation that can misguide early stopping and hurt ARI.

- Avoid prior dir pollution. Keep any provisional gene indices/matrices in-memory or under a temp directory; only write reconciled priors after feature selection. This prevents stale or overly broad priors from persisting across runs and degrading future ARI.

- Add provenance flags. Persist var columns indicating: original_name, canonical_symbol, was_duplicated, is_mito/rRNA/pseudogene, in_any_pathway, in_marker_set, HVG. Also log counts for |HVG|, |prior-only|, |marker-only|, and their overlaps. Use assertions to warn if |feature_genes| ≈ n_genes (catching the feature selection bug early). These diagnostics help tune preprocessing to maximize ARI.

- Optional QC that often lifts ARI:
  - Doublet detection (e.g., Scrublet) to remove high-score cells.
  - Exclude known cell-cycle gene lists from HVGs (keep them for reconstruction but not as HVG drivers).
  - Augment outlier filtering with IQR-based rules in addition to hard cutoffs.</FEEDBACK>


