- Canonical gene mapping and duplicate handling: map to HGNC using meta_info.summary_symbol before QC/HVG; aggregate duplicate symbols by summing counts (not keep-first). This reduces spurious dropouts and stabilizes HVG rankings, improving cluster separation.

- Complete prior curation from resources: actually parse MSigDB/GO/NeST and Cell_marker_Human.xlsx, intersect to dataset symbols, size-filter [10,300], drop sets with >50% missing, and de-duplicate by Jaccard > 0.8 (preferring MSigDB > GO > NeST). Build membership matrices and metadata consistently. ARI improves when z_set aligns to biologically coherent sets.

- Fix set metadata alignment after column pruning: when dropping empty columns from geneset_membership, filter geneset_metadata by the same boolean mask (not by length/head), preserving 1:1 set-column correspondence; otherwise W_path and set labels get misaligned, degrading interpretability and downstream clustering.

- Gene graph correctness: build co-membership Jaccard edges once, then explicitly write undirected edges or ensure the consumer symmetrizes; verify indices map to gene_index order. Misaligned graphs destabilize Laplacian regularization and hurt latent quality.

- Enforce biologically motivated gene filters: in addition to MT- removal and min_cells≥3, drop rRNA and obvious pseudogenes using var['gene_type'] or meta_info.summary_type. Optionally exclude ubiquitous technical families (e.g., IG*, TR*, HLA) unless needed by curated markers. This reduces noise that blurs clusters.

- HVG robustness across batches: with batch_key='domain', prefer selecting genes with highly_variable_nbatches ≥ ceil(n_batches/2) (or use highly_variable_intersection) to avoid domain-specific artifacts. In the fallback path, explicitly use layer='log1p_cpm' to avoid silently using an unintended X. Stable HVGs sharpen cluster boundaries and typically raise ARI.

- Keep raw counts integer and store size factors: keep layers['counts'] as int32; add obs['size_factor'] = nCount_RNA / 1e4 for the decoder. Accurate NB scaling improves reconstruction and latent structure quality, which correlates with better clustering.

- Recompute/validate alignment invariants: after subsetting to feature_genes, assert adata.var_names exactly equals gene_index['genes']; assert geneset_membership row order matches; and ensure gene_weights rows match var_names. Silent misalignments directly degrade ARI.

- Prior-weighting improvements: apply +0.5 weight to marker genes and +0.25 to pathway-specific genes (in ≤2 sets), and hard-cap downweights (MT-, RPL/RPS, MALAT1, low-value) at 0.25. Confirm weights are used by the reconstruction loss. Proper weighting enhances signal from informative genes during training.

- Domain-aware QC edge cases: when domains are highly imbalanced, compute HVGs on a domain-balanced subsample (equal cells per domain) to prevent overrepresentation of one domain’s variability, then apply to full data. This typically yields more generalizable clusters.

- Output consistency to avoid downstream misuse: persist a single adata_preprocessed.h5ad (plus the split column) alongside split files, and ensure prior_config.reconstruction_loss_weights points to the curated gene_weights.csv under the same priors directory. Reduces chances of training with mismatched priors, indirectly protecting ARI.</FEEDBACK>

