Here is the feedback we got for generated code for prior_construction.py in the conversation:

<FEEDBACK>- Align gene universe to what the model actually uses. Build gene_index and all prior artifacts from the preprocessed training features (preprocess_train_mod1.h5ad) or, at minimum, from the union of expressed genes (e.g., detection fraction ≥1–2%) instead of all var_names in the raw RNA_count.h5ad. This removes unexpressed/noise rows from W_path and improves cluster separability (higher ARI).
- Remove version suffixes and harmonize more aggressively before mapping to HGNC (e.g., strip .\d+ transcript versions, replace hyphens/slashes, collapse common alias patterns). This increases overlap with curated resources and strengthens the prior signal.
- Prune low-value genes from curated sets before building B: drop MT-*, RPL*, RPS*, MALAT1, pseudogenes (meta_info), rRNA (meta_info), and non-coding small RNAs (e.g., LINC*, SNORD*, SNORA*) unless they appear in retained marker panels. This prevents W_path from encoding ubiquitous/uninformative features that blur clusters.
- Incorporate expression-aware curation. Compute per-gene detection across cells and:
  - Remove genes with detection fraction below a small threshold from each set.
  - Drop sets whose post-filter size falls outside [10, 300] or whose median detection remains very low. This keeps z_set focused on biologically active pathways in the dataset, improving ARI.
- Sharpen set specificity by handling ubiquitous genes explicitly. Beyond TF-IDF, remove memberships for the top 5–10% most ubiquitous genes (by df_counts) from B (except if in marker sets). This increases discriminative content per latent and reduces leakage across cell types.
- Improve TF-IDF normalization so all sets contribute comparably:
  - Use idf = log((1 + n_sets) / (1 + df)) + 1.
  - L2-normalize columns of B_tfidf. This prevents large/broad sets from dominating z_set magnitudes and typically yields cleaner clusters.
- Equalize column scale of the fixed prior branch explicitly. After constructing B or B_tfidf, rescale columns to unit L1/L2 norm and record the scaling in geneset_metadata.csv. This stabilizes optimization and improves separability in the latent.
- Make ordering and consumption unambiguous. Add set_index (0-based) to geneset_metadata.csv and persist geneset_index.json (list of set_id in column order). This avoids silent column-order mismatches that degrade the prior’s effect on clustering.
- Align prior_config to training hyperparameters used downstream (lambda_graph=1e-3, lambda_residual_l1=5e-4, lambda_marker_exclusivity=1e-3, add lambda_z=1e-4, residual_dim=64). Mismatched strengths dilute regularization benefits and can hurt ARI.
- Build the gene graph with an adaptive threshold targeting a reasonable average degree (e.g., 10–30) and write the chosen threshold to prior_config. Overly sparse or dense graphs weaken the intended Laplacian smoothing’s impact on useful residual structure.
- Tighten gene set de-duplication and quality filters:
  - Prefer MSigDB “Hallmark” and immune/cell-identity collections when present.
  - Downweight or discard GO terms with names matching generic patterns (e.g., “regulation of”, “process”, “metabolic process” without specificity) unless they remain small and specific after expression filtering.
- Improve marker panel construction:
  - Enforce 5–50 markers per cell type after intersecting with expressed genes (detection threshold), prioritizing genes with higher detection and lower global set ubiquity.
  - Optionally group markers into mutually exclusive families (e.g., T vs B vs myeloid) and persist these groups to support stronger exclusivity penalties downstream.
- Persist diagnostics to guide downstream tuning and guardrails:
  - gene_set_frequency.csv (df_counts per gene),
  - coverage_stats.json (kept sets, size distribution, median detection),
  - pruned_genes_blacklist.csv (genes removed from sets). These allow quick iteration on thresholds that most affect ARI.</FEEDBACK>

